Turn the provided monitoring alert into an actionable incident card or runbook. Establish the affected component, user impact, severity, and likely service-level risk from the rule and available operational context.

Put the most useful supported mitigation before deep diagnosis. State its preconditions, target, and effect; do not invent a universally safe restart or rollback when the alert does not justify one. Writing a runbook does not authorize operating the service.

Prefer diagnostics usable from the engineer's local workstation: namespaced kubectl describe, events, current and previous logs, and resource usage. Preserve explicit placeholders for unknown pods, namespaces, clusters, and workloads. Avoid unnecessary pipelines and node SSH assumptions. Include verified Grafana, source-rule, and related-runbook links when available; identify missing links rather than fabricate them. Separate observations, probable causes, suggested commands, and commands actually executed.
